s for forbidden-subdiagram classes; it is stated as open in the source.\n\nThe Catalan-square conjecture for connected chordal diagrams\n\nConjecture openOpen about 6 years · documented by 2020\n\nLet $\\mathcal{C}_{n}(T_{\\geqslant 4},B_{\\geqslant 4})$C n​(T⩾4​,B⩾4​) be the set of connected chord diagrams of size $n$n avoiding top and bottom cycles of size at least $4$4, and let $C_n$C n​ denote the $n$n th Catalan number. The Catalan-square conjecture. The cardinality of this set is\n\n$$\\left|\\mathcal{C}_{n}(T_{\\geqslant 4},B_{\\geqslant 4})\\right|=C_n^2.$$∣C n​(T⩾4​,B⩾4​)∣=C n 2​.\n\nThe claim is one of several experimentally suggested enumerations for forbidden-subdiagram classes; it is stated as open in the source.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Lukas Nabergall, “The combinatorics of a tree-like functional equation for connected chord diagrams”, arXiv:2104.02296 (2021).\n\n## References\n\n2 papers in this index state this conjecture (2020–2021). The statement above is taken from the most recent of them; the others are arXiv:2011.13242.\n\nchord-diagramsenumerative-combinatorics\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 3 days ago\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConnectivity of orientations of 3-edge-connected graphs (https://pagesperso.g-scop.grenoble-inp.fr/florianhoersch/wp-content/uploads/sites/14/2021/01/Connectivity_orientation.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; An odd-vertex pairing of G is a perfect matching in ... orientation of G + P to E(G) yields a well-balanced orientation of G. ... Also, as well-balanced orientations of 2k-edge-connected graphs are k- ... The next one concerns matching theory and was proven by Petersen [9]. ... Further, we mention an intensively studied conjecture which was proposed\n[8].\nTheorem 10. Every 2k-edge-connected graph has k edge-disjoint spanning trees.\nThe next one concerns matching theory and was proven by Petersen [9].\nTheorem 11. Every cubic 2-edge-connected graph has a perfect matching.\nThe next one concerns minimum edge cuts and can for example be found as\nTheorem 7.1.2 in [3].\nProposition 1. Let G be a 3-edge-connected graph and let δ(X) and δ(X′) be\n3-edge-cuts of G. Then δ(X) and δ(X′) are not crossing, i.e. one of X −X′,\nX′ −X, X ∩X′ or V (G) −(X ∪X′) is empty.\nLet G = (V, E) be a graph and T ⊆V . A T-join is deﬁned to be a set F ⊆E\nsuch that the set of odd degree vertices of G(F) is T. We use the following\ncharacterization of the existence of T-joins that can be found as Proposition\n12.7 in [5].\nProposition 2. Let G = (V, E) be a graph and T ⊆V . Then G contains a\nT-join if and only if every connected component of G contains an even number\nof elements of T.\nThe following theorem is due to Menger [6] and is a fundamental character-\nization of k-edge-connected graphs.\nTheorem 12. A graph G = (V, E) is k-edge-connected if and only if λG(u, v) ≥\nk for all u, v ∈V .\nFurther, we mention an intensively studied conjecture which was proposed\nindependently by Berge and Fulkerson [12].\nConjecture 1. Every cubic 2-edge-connected graph has a set of six perfect\nmatchings such that every edge is contained in exactly two of them.\n5--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA318916 - OEIS (https://oeis.org/A318916)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.2 years ago; Crawled: 8.2 years ago; Minimum length of longest palindromic subsequence, where the minimum is taken over all binary circular words of length n. ... Slide 10 of Andrew Ryzhikov's talk contains the conjecture that a(n) >= 3n/4, but this conjecture fails for n = 2 and n = 62 (at least). ... Andrew Ryzhikov, Regular subsequences in finite words, talk for the Workshop on Words and Complexity, 19-23 Feb 2018, Villeurbanne, France. ... A rotation of this circular word gives 0101100, and deleting the first 1 gives the subsequence 001100, which is a palindrome of length 6.\n\nA318916\n\nMinimum length of longest palindromic subsequence, where the minimum is taken over all binary circular words of length n.\n\n0\n\n1, 1, 3, 3, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7, 8, 9, 9, 11, 11, 12, 13, 13, 14, 15, 15, 17, 17, 18, 19, 19, 20, 21, 21, 22, 23, 23, 24\n\n(list; graph; refs; listen; history; text; internal format)\n\nOFFSET\n\n1,3\n\nCOMMENTS\n\nHere \"subsequence\" means \"scattered subsequence\" (not necessarily contiguous). A circular word \"wraps around\".\n\nSlide 10 of Andrew Ryzhikov's talk contains the conjecture that a(n) >= 3n/4, but this conjecture fails for n = 2 and n = 62 (at least). The length-62 counterexample 11111111000000000001010001110110000011010011111001000111010111, where the longest palindromic subsequence is of length 45, was found by Antonio Molina Lovett.\n\n\nTable of n, a(n) for n=1..32.\n\nAndrew Ryzhikov, Regular subsequences in finite words, talk for the Workshop on Words and Complexity, 19-23 Feb 2018, Villeurbanne, France.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nConsider the circular word 0001011. A rotation of this circular word gives 0101100, and deleting the first 1 gives the subsequence 001100, which is a palindrome of length 6. This shows that a(7) >= 6.\n\nCROSSREFS\n\nSequence in context: A054847 A335266 A067782 * A390046 A035299 A338215\n\nAdjacent sequences: A318913 A318914 A318915 * A318917 A318918 A318919\n\nKEYWORD\n\nnonn,more\n\nAUTHOR\n\nJeffrey Shallit, Sep 05 2018\n\nEXTENSIONS\n\nTerms a(19)-a(32) computed by Antonio Molina Lovett\n\nSTATUS\n\napproved\n\n\nLast modified September 9 22:20 EDT 2026. Contains 399097 sequences.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nreposiTUm: Palindromic subsequences in finite words (https://repositum.tuwien.at/handle/20.500.12708/143959)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2.9 years ago; Palindromic subsequences in finite words ... In Language and Automata Theory and Applications  13th International Conference, LATA 2019, St. ... Inproceedings - Full-Paper Contribution ... In 1999 Lyngsø and Pedersen proposed a conjecture stating that every binary circular word of length n with equal number of zeros and ones has an antipalindromic linear subsequence of length at least 23n.\n\nTitle: \n\nPalindromic subsequences in finite words\n\n-\n\nCitation: \n\nMüllner, C., & Ryzhikov, A. (2019). Palindromic subsequences in finite words. In Language and Automata Theory and Applications  13th International Conference, LATA 2019, St. Petersburg, Russia, March 26-29, 2019, Proceedings (pp. 460–468). Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-13435-8_34\n\nPublication Type: \n\nInproceedings - Full-Paper Contribution\n\nen\n\nAuthors: \n\nMüllner, Clemens \nRyzhikov, Andrew \n\nOrganisational Unit: \n\nE104-05 - Forschungsbereich Kombinatorik und Algorithmen \n\n-\n\nPublished in: \n\nLanguage and Automata Theory and Applications 13th International Conference, LATA 2019, St. Petersburg, Russia, March 26-29, 2019, Proceedings \n\nDate (published): \n\n2019\n\n-\n\nEvent name: \n\nLanguage and Automata Theory and Applications 13th International Conference, LATA 2019 \n\n-\n\nEvent date: \n\n26-Mar-2019 - 29-Mar-2019\n\nEvent place: \n\nSt. Petersburg, Russian Federation (the)\n\n-\n\nNumber of Pages: \n\n9\n\n-\n\nPublisher: \n\nSpringer, Cham\n\n-\n\nPeer reviewed: \n\n\nAbstract: \n\nIn 1999 Lyngsø and Pedersen proposed a conjecture stating that every binary circular word of length n with equal number of zeros and ones has an antipalindromic linear subsequence of length at least 23n. No progress over a trivial 12n bound has been achieved since then. We suggest a palindromic counterpart to this conjecture and provide a non-trivial infinite series of circular words which prove the upper bound of 23n for both conjectures at the same time. The construction also works for words over an alphabet of size k and gives rise to a generalization of the conjecture by Lyngsø and Pedersen. Moreover, we discuss some possible strengthenings and weakenings of the named conjectures. We also propose two similar conjectures for linear words and provide some evidences for them.\n\nResearch Areas: \n\naußerhalb der gesamtuniversitären Forschungsschwerpunkte: 100%\n\n-\n\nScience Branch: \n\nMathematik--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPalindromic Subsequences in Finite Words (https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.07502)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.8 years ago; Title: Palindromic Subsequences in Finite Words ... In 1999 Lyngs{\\o} and Pedersen proposed a conjecture stating that every binary circular word of length $n$ with equal number of zeros and ones has an antipalindromic linear subsequence of length at least $\\frac{2}{3}n$.\nTitle: Palindromic Subsequences in Finite Words\nAuthors: Clemens Müllner, Andrew Ryzhikov\nDate: Tue Jan 22 18:24:32 2019\n\nIn 1999 Lyngs{\\o} and Pedersen proposed a conjecture stating that every binary circular word of length $n$ with equal number of zeros and ones has an antipalindromic linear subsequence of length at least $\\frac{2}{3}n$. No progress over a trivial $\\frac{1}{2}n$ bound has been achieved since then. We suggest a palindromic counterpart to this conjecture and provide a non-trivial infinite series of circular words which prove the upper bound of $\\frac{2}{3}n$ for both conjectures at the same time. The construction also works for words over an alphabet of size $k$ and gives rise to a generalization of the conjecture by Lyngs{\\o} and Pedersen. Moreover, we discuss some possible strengthenings and weakenings of the named conjectures. We also propose two similar conjectures for linear words and provide some evidences for them.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEnumeration of Chord Diagrams (https://math.uni.lu/geoquant/vol2